  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual workflow for environmental observing and information delivery across a landscape. It links field sensor networks, aircraft, and satellites to a central system that collects, manages, and analyzes data. At the left and top, “resources,” “sensors,” and “data” feed into stages such as integrated data management, in situ monitoring and remote sensing, and then big-data or cloud-computing methods. These outputs are turned into information through web services and citizen science, and finally distributed to government, researchers, and the public.\n\nThe landscape illustration shows that this observing system spans multiple domains at once, including disaster, ecology, geomorphology, environment, and hydrology. Small ground stations are spread throughout the area, while planes and satellites provide broader observation from above. Dashed blue arrows indicate data flow among sensors, platforms, and processing centers; gray lines show observation angles or method flow; pale shaded areas suggest observation coverage.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes an integrated geospatial monitoring framework: heterogeneous observations from ground to space are combined into shared data products and services that support science, public awareness, and decision-making across environmental applications.",
